## The short version

Ship the package that lets your project outlive the semester: an **as-built architecture overview**, a **setup guide verified by one more cold-eyed stranger** (onboarding test round 2), a **roadmap framed as an invitation**, an **honest known-issues and tech-debt log**, an **onboarding guide** answering a newcomer's five questions, and **at least one automated test** a newcomer can run — plus a **written retrospective** in the repository that checks your original claims against what actually happened, and a short **individual reflection** from each member, submitted privately, on whom the work served. Tag the repo. That's launch.

---

## Why this assignment exists

A semester project usually dies the day grades post — not because the code stops working, but because everything needed to continue it lived in its authors' heads. The launch package is the antidote: the founder's final responsibility of **writing the project down well enough that it can be picked up** — by a capstone team, a successor, a stranger on the internet, or you-in-eight-months, who is functionally a stranger too. This is the "lasting" half of the course's promise made real: projects built to last are projects whose knowledge survives their builders' attention.

The retrospective trains a rarer and more valuable skill: **checking your own claims against evidence, in writing, without spin.** Fourteen weeks ago you claimed a need was real and a scope was feasible; now you have data. The discipline of writing "we assumed X; here's what happened; here's what we learned" — blamelessly, per the postmortem culture the best engineering organizations run on — is what turns experience into judgment. Note what the rubric grades: **the evidence, not the verdict.** A claim that held, shown with evidence and with an account of why the call was right, was good judgment. A claim that didn't hold, documented with what you learned, is discovery working. Both earn full credit; there's no quota of failures to report. People and organizations that can do this improve; those that can't repeat themselves. It is also, plainly, the most credible artifact you'll show capstone faculty — and honesty here is verified live in Demo Day Q&A.

