## Tips and common pitfalls

- **The package is assembly, not invention.** Almost everything exists in draft — the work is refresh, verify, and make true.
- **The as-built pass matters most on the architecture doc:** stale diagrams are worse than no diagrams, because a newcomer trusts them.
- **Spin runs both ways.** Success claimed without evidence and setbacks listed to look reflective are the same mistake. Don't go looking for failures to report: if a call was right, say why it was right and whether you'd make it the same way again.
- **"Held" means it could have failed.** A claim held if something could have shown it wrong and didn't (your first-version falsifier, a user who could have said no). A claim nothing ever tested is *open*. Say so.
- **A newcomer-facing gap can become a `good first issue`** — that's not cheating; that's the system working.
- **Ask the January question:** "if someone forked this with no one to ask — what kills them first?" Fix or log that item today.

